\begin{document}

\title{Secure User Authentication Scheme Using Multimodal Biometrics}

% Double-blind submission: author names and the institution are withheld, and
% the authors' own earlier work is cited in the third person throughout.
\author{Anonymous Author(s)}
\institute{Submitted for double-blind review}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Authentication systems increasingly require stronger protection against credential theft, replay, and biometric spoofing while remaining practical to deploy. Although multimodal biometrics can improve authentication robustness, conventional approaches often require separate acquisition of multiple traits, increasing hardware requirements, cost, and user interaction. This paper presents a deployable multimodal authentication system that derives face, voice, and a dynamic challenge response factor from a single real time video recording. During login, the user records a video while responding to a server issued CAPTCHA. Face verification and speaker verification are performed using learned biometric embeddings and cosine similarity based decisions, while the spoken response is transcribed and validated against the session specific challenge. The three factors are evaluated concurrently and combined using a conjunctive decision rule, granting access only when all verification factors succeed.

The system therefore enables single sensor acquisition of multiple authentication factors without requiring dedicated biometric hardware for each modality. For privacy, uploaded recordings are reduced to templates and are not retained as persistent media, the resulting templates are encrypted under per user keys, and the corresponding key material is held in a separate vault, so recovering a template requires compromising both stores. Speech to text is performed on premises, so the login audio does not leave the deployment.
%
% Measured claims. The macros are written by the evaluation and attack
% harnesses; the \else branches state plainly that a measurement is outstanding
% rather than leaving a number-shaped gap in the sentence.
\ifresultsready
We evaluate recognition accuracy on \EvalCorpusName, forming genuine pairs only across different sessions of the same subject (\EvalGenuinePairs{} pairs) and impostor pairs across different subjects (\EvalImpostorPairs{} pairs), and report an Equal Error Rate (EER) of \EvalFaceEER\% for the face factor and \EvalVoiceEER\% for the speaker factor, together with False Acceptance Rate (FAR) and False Rejection Rate (FRR) at the deployed operating points and the measured cost of the challenge factor's own false rejections (\EvalChallengeFRR\%).
\else
Recognition accuracy is evaluated on a public audio visual corpus using an automated harness that applies the deployed matcher paths, forms genuine pairs only across different sessions of the same subject and impostor pairs across different subjects, and reports FAR, FRR, EER, ROC, DET and AUC; the corpus run is outstanding and no accuracy figure is claimed here.
\fi
\ifattacksready
Against presentation attacks the system is evaluated directly: replay of archived recordings, face--voice splicing in both directions, photograph and screen replay, synthetic speech, and face swapping are each mounted against the deployed decision rule and reported as attack success rates, per factor and for the system as a whole.
\else
A presentation attack harness mounts replay, face--voice splicing in both directions, photograph and screen replay, synthetic speech and face swapping against the deployed decision rule, reporting each as an attack success rate per factor and for the system; those runs are outstanding.
\fi
The evaluation also identifies template extraction and model loading as the main contributors to authentication latency. The architecture is presented together with an explicit adversary model and a statement of the attacks it does not defend against, notably real time relay and real time responsive deepfakes.

\keywords{Multimodal biometrics \and Authentication \and Challenge response \and Face verification \and Speaker verification \and Replay resistance \and Template protection}
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Nomenclature:} FAR, False Acceptance Rate; FRR, False Rejection Rate; EER, Equal Error Rate; ROC, Receiver Operating Characteristic; DET, Detection Error Tradeoff; AUC, Area Under the Curve; ASR, Attack Success Rate; PAD, Presentation Attack Detection; STT, Speech to Text; TTL, Time to Live; MITM, Man in the Middle.

\section {Introduction}

\subsection{Authentication}

Authentication is the process of verifying the identity of an entity before granting access to a system, service, or resource. In user authentication, the user presents one or more credentials that are verified by the system. Authentication factors are commonly classified as knowledge, possession, and inherence factors. Knowledge factors include passwords and other information known to the user, possession factors include physical devices or tokens such as mobile phones and smart cards, and inherence factors are based on biometric characteristics such as fingerprints, facial features, iris patterns, or voice.

This work focuses on user to device authentication, in which a user is authenticated before being granted access to a protected resource. Traditional authentication systems commonly rely on passwords or other knowledge based credentials. However, such credentials may be exposed, guessed, stolen, or reused. Biometric authentication instead uses characteristics associated with the individual and can therefore provide an additional basis for identity verification. Combining multiple biometric traits can further reduce reliance on a single characteristic.

The proposed work uses face and voice as biometric factors and combines them with a dynamic challenge response mechanism. The user submits a real time recording from which the biometric and challenge response factors are derived, and access is granted only when all required verification decisions succeed. This motivates the multimodal authentication architecture presented in the following sections.

\subsection{Cataloguing of Authentication Schemes}

Authentication schemes can be broadly classified according to the number and type of credentials used for verification, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:2}. Single factor authentication relies on one credential, whereas two factor and multi factor authentication combine multiple factors to provide additional layers of verification. Authentication factors are commonly drawn from knowledge, possession, and inherence categories.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{Picture2.png}
\caption{Cataloguing of Authentication Schemes}
\label{fig:2}
\end{figure*}

Biometric authentication belongs to the inherence category and uses physiological or behavioural characteristics to verify identity. Examples include fingerprints, facial features, iris patterns, voice, and gait. Conventional unimodal biometric systems rely on a single trait and may be affected by variations in sensing conditions, data quality, or limited discriminative information. Multimodal biometric systems address these limitations by combining multiple biometric traits, providing the motivation for the face and voice based authentication architecture proposed in this work.

This paper focuses on multimodal biometric authentication rather than providing a detailed taxonomy of password, possession based, or graphical authentication schemes.
\subsection{Research problem and motivation}
Researchers are trying to find a more secure and robust authentication mechanism to combat security threats and prevent the system from cyber attacks. Single factor authentication can be vulnerable to compromise of its single authentication factor, motivating the use of multiple complementary factors. Passwords may be exposed, guessed, stolen, or reused through attacks such as guessing and brute force attacks. MFA  and passwordless authentication is gaining popularity and becoming the current trend. In the previous subsection, the benefits of biometric authentication are discussed and how multimodal biometric authentication can be a viable solution. Multimodal biometrics, which involves more than one biometric trait, is a secure and effective authentication mode. All the schemes based on multimodal biometrics involve some problems like
\begin{itemize}
\item interacting with different devices to provide biometric identity
\item The extra cost involved in purchasing devices
\end{itemize}
So, \textcolor{black}{many multimodal biometric systems use separate acquisition modalities or dedicated sensing hardware, which can increase deployment complexity and cost. The proposed scheme instead combines parallel acquisition of biometrics from a commodity camera and microphone with binding of that acquisition to a fresh server issued challenge}.

{\color{black}
\subsection{Major Contributions}\label{sec:contrib}
Below are the main contributions of this work. First, we present a framework for passwordless multimodal biometric authentication that combines facial and speaker recognition with a dynamic challenge response mechanism within a single authentication session. To prevent successful authentication through the compromise of a single authentication factor, the proposed framework employs a strict AND based fusion strategy, requiring the simultaneous verification of the facial biometric, speaker biometric, and session specific challenge response. Second, we introduce a server generated, unpredictable, and single use dynamic CAPTCHA as a challenge response mechanism to mitigate the reuse of previously recorded authentication media. Since each challenge is associated with a distinct authentication attempt, a response recorded during a previous session cannot satisfy the challenge issued in a subsequent session. Third, the proposed architecture reduces reliance on a strictly sequential authentication pipeline by concurrently evaluating the biometric and challenge response factors and applying a unified authentication decision after verification. Fourth, the framework adopts a privacy oriented biometric data management approach in which raw enrollment and authentication recordings are processed transiently and are not retained as permanent biometric records, while facial and speaker representations are extracted as templates and protected using authenticated encryption. Finally, the system incorporates purpose bound enrollment authorization and identity binding. Normal enrollment cannot overwrite an existing template, while authenticated re enrollment can replace it only after successful three factor verification.
The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section~II reviews the related literature, and Section~III presents the proposed multimodal biometric authentication system and its methodology. A comparison with recent authentication systems follows the proposed system discussion. Section~IV reports the evaluation and results, Section~V outlines future work, and Section~VI concludes the paper.
}

\section{Related Work}
Biometric authentication has evolved from single trait verification towards multi factor and multimodal approaches that combine biometric information with additional security mechanisms. Marasco and Albanese \cite{IEEEtran37} proposed FingerPIN, a multi factor authentication approach that combines knowledge and inherence factors through a fingerprint based personal identification number (PIN), while an improved version of the scheme was subsequently introduced to strengthen security and usability \cite{IEEEtran38}. Li and Hwang \cite{IEEEtran32} proposed a remote biometric authentication scheme based on biometric verification, one way hashing, and smart card technology. Das \cite{IEEEtran33} later proposed an improved scheme, however, it was shown to lack resistance against offline password guessing and privileged insider attacks and did not provide user anonymity. To address these limitations, Lee and Hsu \cite{IEEEtran56} introduced an alternative approach based on extended chaotic maps.

Other studies have investigated the integration of biometric traits with cryptographic mechanisms and machine learning techniques. Jagadiswary and Saraswady \cite{IEEEtran58} proposed an authentication scheme combining RSA asymmetric cryptography with biometric traits such as fingerprints, retina scans, and finger-vein patterns. Finger vein based authentication and identification have also received attention, including approaches focused on improving vein image quality and reducing noise during feature extraction \cite{IEEEtran61}, as well as convolutional neural network based biometric identification systems \cite{IEEEtran62}. These studies demonstrate the applicability of cryptographic and learning based techniques for strengthening biometric authentication, however, they primarily focus on different biometric modalities and acquisition settings from those considered in the present work.

More recent research has increasingly explored multimodal authentication through different combinations of biometric traits and fusion strategies. Hammad et al.\ \cite{r1} proposed sequential and parallel multimodal authentication systems using ECG and fingerprint biometrics and employed CNN and Q-Gaussian multi support vector machine (QG-MSVM) based processing. Kim and Hong \cite{r4} combined teeth images and voice biometrics, where individual matching scores were integrated using weighted score level fusion. Ahamed et al.\ \cite{r2} proposed an AI based device level authentication model using ECG and PPG signals, while \cite{r3} investigated the combination of fingerprint, face, and iris biometrics using different machine learning based fusion and classification approaches. Collectively, these studies demonstrate the benefits of combining multiple biometric traits, however, they differ in terms of the biometric modalities considered, acquisition requirements, fusion strategies, and security mechanisms.

The proposed work differs from these approaches by integrating facial verification, speaker verification, and a session specific spoken challenge within a unified passwordless authentication framework. Rather than relying solely on biometric score fusion, the system independently verifies the facial biometric, speaker biometric, and challenge response and applies a strict decision level AND rule for the final authentication decision. The dynamic challenge additionally mitigates the reuse of previously recorded authentication media by requiring a response to a fresh challenge associated with the current authentication attempt. The proposed architecture also incorporates concurrent factor evaluation, privacy oriented template only storage with authenticated encryption, and an authorized enrollment mechanism. A more detailed comparison with recent end to end biometric authentication systems follows the proposed system discussion and is summarized in Table~\ref{tab:recentsec}.

