{\color{black}
\subsection{\textcolor{black}{Threat Model}}\label{sec:threat}
\paragraph{Adversary capabilities.} The adversary is assumed to possess archived genuine face and voice media obtained from public sources or previous sessions, to control or modify the client device, to observe or manipulate the network path, and to attempt phishing, relay, and replay based presentation attacks. Face and voice are observable biometric traits rather than secrets. Security therefore does not rely on biometric secrecy; it relies on a fresh server generated challenge, protection of stored templates and key material, and conjunctive verification of all three factors.

\paragraph{Trust assumptions.} The verifier, server side challenge generator, account store, template store, and separate key vault are trusted to execute the specified protocol and protect their server side secrets. The challenge is unpredictable, expires after five minutes, and is consumed regardless of the authentication outcome. The server is also trusted to enforce purpose bound enrollment and re enrollment grants and to return an identical client response for factor failures.

\paragraph{Out of scope.} A real time relay that presents the live challenge to the victim and returns the response within the validity window is out of scope. A real time responsive deepfake capable of generating the victim's face and voice while speaking an unpredictable challenge is also out of scope, as are verifier compromise, key vault compromise, coercion of the enrolled user, and operator abuse. The scheme therefore claims resistance to straightforward replay of previously captured media, but does not claim protection against these out of scope attacks.
}

\technicalsubsections
\renewcommand{\algorithmicrequire}{\textbf{Input:}}
\renewcommand{\algorithmicensure}{\textbf{Output:}}

\recentcomparison

\subsection{Account Lifecycle}\label{sec:lifecycle}
The deployed system implements the following account lifecycle.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Account creation and enrollment, implemented.} A purpose bound \texttt{enroll} grant authorizes initial template creation for the named account. Normal enrollment cannot overwrite an existing enrolled template. Username and email uniqueness are enforced, while biometric uniqueness across accounts is not.
\item \textbf{Account update, implemented.} The authenticated \texttt{PUT /api/updateuser} operation updates account information. Changed personally identifiable information is sealed again, and a changed email address receives a new keyed index after collision checking.
\item \textbf{Authenticated re enrollment, implemented.} \texttt{POST /api/reenroll-request} is answered only after the same successful face, voice, and challenge verification used for login. The resulting purpose bound \texttt{reenroll} grant is valid for ten minutes and may replace only an existing biometric template. Replacement uses a newly generated per user key and an atomic ciphertext swap; failure before completion preserves the prior enrollment.
\item \textbf{Account deletion and biometric erasure, implemented.} The authenticated \texttt{DELETE /api/account} operation removes the account record, encrypted biometric template, separate vault key entry, and any transient login recording associated with the account.
\item \textbf{Operator recovery, administrative procedure.} If a user cannot complete three factor authentication, recovery is not exposed as a public API endpoint. An operator verifies identity out of band, deletes the existing account through the administrative equivalent of the erasure procedure, and permits the user to register and enroll again. This administrative process is the explicit recovery trust anchor.
\end{itemize}

\section{Evaluation and Results}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
A proof of concept web application was implemented to validate the feasibility of the proposed scheme under real time acquisition. The prototype follows a client--server architecture in which the client performs media acquisition and displays the server issued challenge, while enrolment processing, challenge generation, feature extraction, matching, and decision fusion are performed on the server. Keeping the decision logic server side prevents a modified client from directly bypassing authentication checks.

\subsubsection{Hardware Requirements}\label{sec:hw}
Functional verification was performed on a machine equipped with an Intel Core i5-4210U processor and 8\,GB of RAM. The processing-time measurements reported in Section~\ref{sec:perf} were collected on a separate machine equipped with an Intel Core i9-12900 processor and 62\,GB of RAM. The reported timing results should therefore be interpreted with respect to the second machine.

\subsubsection{Prototype Architecture}
The implementation uses a React single page client, an Express server on Node.js~22, MongoDB for account and storage data, and Python~3.12 for the biometric pipeline. Face inference is performed through ONNX Runtime, while speaker processing uses PyTorch. Production deployments require independent secrets for token signing, biometric storage, and account/PII protection. The server validates these secrets at startup and rejects missing, insecure, or improperly shared production configuration.

{\color{black}

\subsection{\textcolor{black}{Processing and Storage Footprint}}\label{sec:perf}
