Login template extraction is the dominant processing stage because it loads and executes both the face and speaker model stacks. Model loading contributes substantially to this overhead. By comparison, face and voice matching require only similarity computations between extracted and enrolled representations and introduce comparatively low overhead.

\emph{Storage footprint.} The persistent per user biometric artefact is approximately 147\,KB, while the source recording used for this measurement was 11.18\,MB, an approximately 76 fold difference for that recording. The ratio depends on recording duration, resolution, frame rate, and codec and is therefore reported only as a deployment storage measurement, not as a generic security, accuracy, or comparative advantage. Template storage is $O(1)$ with respect to recording length because the stored representation has fixed dimensions: twelve 512 dimensional face embeddings, one 512 dimensional mean face reference, and one 192 dimensional speaker embedding. The AES 256 GCM envelope adds a fixed 34 byte overhead. Network upload time was not measured and is therefore not reported as an experimental result.

\subsubsection{Matcher Diagnostics and Threshold Provenance}
The implemented recognition evaluation harness comprises \texttt{evaluate\_corpus.py}, the \texttt{run\_evaluation.mjs} driver, and the collection and execution protocol in \texttt{docs/EVALUATION.md}. It applies the deployed face and voice matcher paths to a labelled corpus, forms genuine pairs only between different sessions of the same subject, forms impostor pairs between different subjects, and can produce score distributions, Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curves, Detection Error Tradeoff (DET) plots, Area Under the Curve (AUC), and Equal Error Rate estimates. The harness is ready for a sufficiently large, separately consented corpus, but such a corpus has not yet been collected and the harness has therefore not produced reportable recognition accuracy results.

For a score threshold $\tau$, the False Acceptance Rate is $\mathrm{FAR}(\tau)=P(s\geq\tau\mid\text{impostor})$, and the False Rejection Rate is $\mathrm{FRR}(\tau)=P(s<\tau\mid\text{genuine})$. The Equal Error Rate is the error value at the threshold where FAR and FRR are equal. These definitions describe the planned evaluation and are not measurements from the present recordings.

The available diagnostic observations showed a face genuine median score of 0.8385 and an impostor median score of 0.0928 at the inherited operating point $\tau_f=0.35$. For voice, the available diagnostic observations showed a genuine median score of 0.455 and an impostor median score of 0.090 at the empirical operating point $\tau_a=0.30$. These observations support operating point discussion but do not constitute a formal FAR, FRR, EER, ROC, DET, or AUC evaluation.

