\subsection{\textcolor{black}{Evaluation Scope and Limitations}}\label{sec:limits}
The evaluation measured processing time, storage footprint, diagnostic matcher score behaviour, and the end to end functional behaviour of the deployed authentication workflow.

No formal cross device evaluation has yet been performed. Although the capture client requests ideal 720p video at 30 frames per second with mono audio and records negotiated device metadata, all current measurements should be interpreted as same environment deployment observations rather than evidence of cross device robustness.

No formal recognition accuracy evaluation was performed. The available corpus is too small to support meaningful estimates of False Acceptance Rate, False Rejection Rate, Equal Error Rate, or ROC based performance. The deployed face and voice thresholds are operating points rather than calibrations against a target error rate.

Enrollment quality is now gated by minimum face size and a minimum number of usable sampled frames. However, template only storage means that discarded enrollment recordings cannot be retrospectively recomputed or re scored. Future recognition accuracy analysis therefore requires a separately collected, consented, multi subject corpus with multiple sessions and devices. The existing evaluation harness can process such a corpus when it becomes available.

\subsubsection{Planned Recognition Accuracy Evaluation}
No sufficiently large consented corpus has yet been collected, so formal recognition accuracy values are not available. When such a corpus is collected, genuine pairs will be formed only between different sessions of the same subject, while impostor pairs will be formed between different subjects. The planned evaluation will report genuine and impostor pair counts, EER, AUC, FAR and FRR at the deployed thresholds, and the threshold at EER for the face, voice, challenge, and fused decisions. These metrics remain future evaluation outputs rather than results of the present study.

\paragraph{Key rotation limitation.}
Key rotation is not yet automated and remains an operational limitation. The system separates signing, biometric storage, and account data protection keys, but replacing a storage key currently requires controlled migration of the corresponding protected data. Future work should provide automated key versioning and online re-encryption to support rotation without service interruption.

}
\section{Future Work}
A primary direction for future work is to strengthen the proposed authentication system against deepfake and advanced presentation attacks. Although the dynamic CAPTCHA provides challenge response based protection against simple replay attacks, an attacker may potentially use AI generated or manipulated facial and voice content to imitate an enrolled user. Therefore, future work will investigate the integration of dedicated deepfake and presentation attack detection mechanisms for both facial and voice modalities.

For the facial modality, future work can incorporate face anti spoofing and deepfake detection techniques to distinguish genuine live facial input from replayed, manipulated, or synthetically generated video. Similarly, for the voice modality, synthetic speech and voice cloning detection can be integrated to identify AI generated or converted speech. These additional checks can operate alongside the existing face verification, speaker verification, and challenge response mechanisms, providing an additional layer of protection rather than replacing the current authentication factors.

Future work will also focus on evaluating the complete system against a broader range of attack scenarios involving manipulated video, cloned speech, and multimodal deepfakes. The goal will be to determine whether face, voice, and liveness related evidence can be jointly analysed to improve resistance against attacks in which multiple biometric modalities are synthetically generated or manipulated simultaneously.

In addition, a larger multi subject evaluation corpus will be collected to measure recognition performance using metrics such as false acceptance rate, false rejection rate, and equal error rate. Further work will also investigate reducing the overhead of template extraction through persistent model services and replacing the external speech to text dependency with an on premises solution to improve privacy and reduce reliance on third party services.

A cross device protocol will record each consenting subject in multiple sessions on at least two devices and will report within device and cross device genuine score distributions, impostor distributions, FAR, FRR, EER, ROC, DET, and AUC results produced by the existing evaluation harness. This evaluation is future work and has not yet been run. Automated key rotation and online re encryption also remain future operational work. No dedicated facial or voice deepfake detector, new Presentation Attack Detection model, or automated key rotation mechanism is claimed as part of the current implementation.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper presents a single sensor multimodal biometric authentication system that combines face verification, speaker verification, and a dynamic challenge response mechanism using a single real time video recording. Facial and voice information are acquired simultaneously, while the user responds to a server generated CAPTCHA. Authentication is granted only when all three factors are successfully verified through a conjunctive decision rule.

The implemented system uses deep face embeddings and cosine similarity for face verification, ECAPA-TDNN speaker embeddings and cosine similarity for speaker verification, and speech to text based challenge validation. Raw enrollment recordings are deleted after template extraction, while encrypted login recordings are retained only transiently and deleted when the authentication attempt resolves. The resulting biometric templates are protected and stored separately from account information, supporting a privacy conscious authentication workflow.

The evaluation measured controlled processing time on the timing machine and reported additional end to end workflow observations from the deployed development system, including registration, successful login, and failed login attempts. Template extraction remained the dominant processing stage, while face and voice matching introduced comparatively low overhead. Recognition accuracy was not statistically characterised because the available corpus was too small for meaningful estimates of metrics such as false acceptance and false rejection rates.

Overall, the proposed system demonstrates a deployable and practical multimodal authentication architecture using commonly available camera and microphone hardware while reducing persistent storage of raw biometric media. It provides a foundation for more robust and privacy conscious multimodal authentication systems.

